1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,858
Sweetheart, on Christmas Eve of nineteen sixty-nine,

2
00:00:03,858 --> 00:00:06,381
I had exactly twenty dollars in my

3
00:00:06,381 --> 00:00:06,826
purse.

4
00:00:07,006 --> 00:00:08,734
It was all the money we had

5
00:00:08,734 --> 00:00:11,871
for Christmas dinner and the children's presents.

6
00:00:12,051 --> 00:00:15,220
And in the Greyhound station in Mansfield,

7
00:00:15,220 --> 00:00:17,258
at eleven o'clock at night,

8
00:00:17,258 --> 00:00:19,295
I gave every penny of it to

9
00:00:19,295 --> 00:00:21,710
a young soldier I had never seen

10
00:00:21,710 --> 00:00:23,069
before in my life.

11
00:00:23,249 --> 00:00:25,876
My husband didn't speak to me for

12
00:00:25,876 --> 00:00:26,592
two days.

13
00:00:26,942 --> 00:00:29,376
Tonight I'm going to tell you why

14
00:00:29,376 --> 00:00:30,039
I did it,

15
00:00:30,039 --> 00:00:32,178
what that soldier said to me,

16
00:00:32,178 --> 00:00:34,390
and what pulled up in front of

17
00:00:34,390 --> 00:00:36,823
our house exactly one year later,

18
00:00:36,823 --> 00:00:39,551
on Christmas Eve of nineteen seventy.

19
00:00:39,731 --> 00:00:40,771
Stay with me,

20
00:00:40,771 --> 00:00:44,053
honey, because I didn't believe it myself

21
00:00:44,053 --> 00:00:45,733
until I saw his face.

22
00:00:45,913 --> 00:00:48,085
And I still keep what he left

23
00:00:48,085 --> 00:00:49,582
on my kitchen table.

24
00:00:49,931 --> 00:00:51,857
That was a hard year for us,

25
00:00:51,857 --> 00:00:52,613
sweetheart.

26
00:00:52,793 --> 00:00:55,387
Harold had been laid off from the

27
00:00:55,387 --> 00:00:56,724
plant in October,

28
00:00:56,724 --> 00:00:58,925
and he was working odd jobs,

29
00:00:58,925 --> 00:01:02,383
shoveling snow and fixing furnaces for cash.

30
00:01:02,563 --> 00:01:04,649
We had two little ones,

31
00:01:04,649 --> 00:01:06,009
Susan and Mark,

32
00:01:06,009 --> 00:01:07,370
five and three.

33
00:01:07,550 --> 00:01:09,256
I was taking in sewing.

34
00:01:09,436 --> 00:01:12,060
Every week, we counted every penny on

35
00:01:12,060 --> 00:01:13,336
the kitchen table,

36
00:01:13,336 --> 00:01:15,746
and every week there were fewer of

37
00:01:15,746 --> 00:01:16,101
them.

38
00:01:16,450 --> 00:01:17,785
By Christmas Eve,

39
00:01:17,785 --> 00:01:20,296
I had saved twenty dollars in an

40
00:01:20,296 --> 00:01:22,337
envelope in my sewing box.

41
00:01:22,517 --> 00:01:24,456
Twenty dollars, honey.

42
00:01:24,636 --> 00:01:26,461
In nineteen sixty-nine,

43
00:01:26,461 --> 00:01:27,651
that was a ham,

44
00:01:27,651 --> 00:01:29,158
a sack of potatoes,

45
00:01:29,158 --> 00:01:31,063
a pie, a doll for Susan,

46
00:01:31,063 --> 00:01:33,522
and a little red fire truck for

47
00:01:33,522 --> 00:01:35,665
Mark that I'd had my eye on

48
00:01:35,665 --> 00:01:38,521
at the five-and-dime for two months.

49
00:01:38,701 --> 00:01:40,620
I had it all planned to the

50
00:01:40,620 --> 00:01:41,046
penny.

51
00:01:41,396 --> 00:01:42,911
But on Christmas Eve,

52
00:01:42,911 --> 00:01:44,136
my mother called.

53
00:01:44,316 --> 00:01:46,692
My father had fallen on the ice

54
00:01:46,692 --> 00:01:48,148
and broken his hip,

55
00:01:48,148 --> 00:01:50,676
and she needed me at the hospital

56
00:01:50,676 --> 00:01:51,596
in Columbus.

57
00:01:51,776 --> 00:01:54,168
Harold stayed with the children,

58
00:01:54,168 --> 00:01:56,485
and I took the evening bus with

59
00:01:56,485 --> 00:01:58,279
my envelope in my purse,

60
00:01:58,279 --> 00:02:00,671
because I planned to shop at the

61
00:02:00,671 --> 00:02:02,689
big stores on the way home.

62
00:02:02,869 --> 00:02:04,795
In a minute I'll tell you why

63
00:02:04,795 --> 00:02:06,654
I never got to those stores.

64
00:02:07,004 --> 00:02:08,976
My father was all right,

65
00:02:08,976 --> 00:02:10,209
thank the Lord.

66
00:02:10,389 --> 00:02:11,649
On the way home,

67
00:02:11,649 --> 00:02:14,407
I had to change buses in Mansfield,

68
00:02:14,407 --> 00:02:17,006
and the station was nearly empty.

69
00:02:17,186 --> 00:02:19,326
Just me, an old man asleep on

70
00:02:19,326 --> 00:02:21,096
a bench, and a boy in an

71
00:02:21,096 --> 00:02:23,899
army uniform sitting by himself in the

72
00:02:23,899 --> 00:02:26,628
corner, with a duffel bag between his

73
00:02:26,628 --> 00:02:26,997
feet.

74
00:02:27,177 --> 00:02:28,591
He was crying,

75
00:02:28,591 --> 00:02:29,197
honey.

76
00:02:29,377 --> 00:02:30,838
Trying hard not to,

77
00:02:30,838 --> 00:02:32,454
the way young men do.

78
00:02:32,634 --> 00:02:33,911
But crying.

79
00:02:34,260 --> 00:02:35,352
I'm a mother.

80
00:02:35,532 --> 00:02:36,879
I couldn't stand it.

81
00:02:37,059 --> 00:02:39,191
So I went over and sat down

82
00:02:39,191 --> 00:02:40,850
next to him and said,

83
00:02:40,850 --> 00:02:42,666
son, are you all right?

84
00:02:42,846 --> 00:02:45,818
He wiped his face fast and said,

85
00:02:45,818 --> 00:02:47,769
yes, ma'am, I'm fine.

86
00:02:47,949 --> 00:02:50,402
And then he told me everything,

87
00:02:50,402 --> 00:02:51,352
all at once,

88
00:02:51,352 --> 00:02:54,121
the way people do with strangers at

89
00:02:54,121 --> 00:02:54,834
midnight.

90
00:02:55,014 --> 00:02:56,755
His name was Billy.

91
00:02:56,935 --> 00:02:58,386
He was nineteen.

92
00:02:58,566 --> 00:03:01,278
And he had just gotten home from

93
00:03:01,278 --> 00:03:01,956
Vietnam.

94
00:03:02,306 --> 00:03:04,525
He'd been wounded in the leg in

95
00:03:04,525 --> 00:03:07,173
October, and he'd spent two months in

96
00:03:07,173 --> 00:03:08,390
an army hospital.

97
00:03:08,570 --> 00:03:11,438
They'd let him go home for Christmas.

98
00:03:11,618 --> 00:03:14,872
But somewhere between California and Ohio,

99
00:03:14,872 --> 00:03:17,894
somebody had stolen his wallet while he

100
00:03:17,894 --> 00:03:19,211
slept on the bus.

101
00:03:19,391 --> 00:03:20,586
All his money.

102
00:03:20,766 --> 00:03:22,298
His ticket to Akron,

103
00:03:22,298 --> 00:03:25,515
where his mother and little sisters lived.

104
00:03:25,695 --> 00:03:28,059
And the last bus to Akron left

105
00:03:28,059 --> 00:03:29,399
in forty minutes.

106
00:03:29,748 --> 00:03:32,099
He said, I called my mom from

107
00:03:32,099 --> 00:03:33,233
the pay phone,

108
00:03:33,233 --> 00:03:33,882
collect.

109
00:03:34,062 --> 00:03:36,314
She doesn't have the money either.

110
00:03:36,494 --> 00:03:39,141
She's been working two jobs since my

111
00:03:39,141 --> 00:03:39,803
dad died.

112
00:03:39,983 --> 00:03:42,410
I told her I'd make it home

113
00:03:42,410 --> 00:03:43,129
somehow.

114
00:03:43,309 --> 00:03:44,810
And then he said,

115
00:03:44,810 --> 00:03:46,135
I promised her,

116
00:03:46,135 --> 00:03:46,664
ma'am.

117
00:03:46,844 --> 00:03:49,007
I promised her I'd be home for

118
00:03:49,007 --> 00:03:51,961
Christmas, every letter I wrote from over

119
00:03:51,961 --> 00:03:52,394
there.

120
00:03:52,574 --> 00:03:54,722
That's what kept me going.

121
00:03:54,902 --> 00:03:57,026
And now I'm going to break it.

122
00:03:57,376 --> 00:03:59,998
If you're enjoying Grandma's story,

123
00:03:59,998 --> 00:04:02,021
tap the like button for me,

124
00:04:02,021 --> 00:04:02,845
sweetheart.

125
00:04:03,025 --> 00:04:06,229
It helps more people find these stories.

126
00:04:06,409 --> 00:04:08,416
I sat there with my purse on

127
00:04:08,416 --> 00:04:10,280
my lap and my hand on that

128
00:04:10,280 --> 00:04:10,925
envelope.

129
00:04:11,105 --> 00:04:12,406
Twenty dollars.

130
00:04:12,586 --> 00:04:13,596
The ham.

131
00:04:13,776 --> 00:04:14,763
The doll.

132
00:04:14,943 --> 00:04:16,672
The red fire truck.

133
00:04:16,852 --> 00:04:18,606
I thought about Harold.

134
00:04:18,786 --> 00:04:20,993
I thought about my babies' faces on

135
00:04:20,993 --> 00:04:23,894
Christmas morning with nothing under the tree.

136
00:04:24,074 --> 00:04:26,303
And then I thought about Billy's mother.

137
00:04:26,653 --> 00:04:28,049
I opened my purse,

138
00:04:28,049 --> 00:04:29,756
took out the envelope,

139
00:04:29,756 --> 00:04:32,082
and put the whole thing in his

140
00:04:32,082 --> 00:04:32,470
hand.

141
00:04:32,650 --> 00:04:35,542
The ticket to Akron was four dollars

142
00:04:35,542 --> 00:04:36,667
and ten cents.

143
00:04:36,847 --> 00:04:38,261
I said, Billy,

144
00:04:38,261 --> 00:04:39,877
buy your ticket.

145
00:04:40,057 --> 00:04:42,439
And use the rest to bring something

146
00:04:42,439 --> 00:04:44,957
home to your mother and your sisters.

147
00:04:45,137 --> 00:04:46,855
He tried to give it back.

148
00:04:47,035 --> 00:04:48,928
He said, ma'am,

149
00:04:48,928 --> 00:04:49,937
I can't.

150
00:04:50,117 --> 00:04:51,859
I can tell this is a lot

151
00:04:51,859 --> 00:04:53,020
of money to you.

152
00:04:53,200 --> 00:04:54,349
And I said,

153
00:04:54,349 --> 00:04:56,439
it's Christmas, son.

154
00:04:56,619 --> 00:04:57,501
Go home.

155
00:04:57,851 --> 00:05:00,522
He asked for my name and address.

156
00:05:00,702 --> 00:05:03,467
He said, I'm going to pay you

157
00:05:03,467 --> 00:05:05,183
back, every penny.

158
00:05:05,363 --> 00:05:07,236
And I wrote it on the back

159
00:05:07,236 --> 00:05:08,533
of his bus ticket,

160
00:05:08,533 --> 00:05:10,550
but I didn't expect a thing,

161
00:05:10,550 --> 00:05:10,982
honey.

162
00:05:11,162 --> 00:05:13,228
I watched him limp out to that

163
00:05:13,228 --> 00:05:14,881
bus with his duffel bag,

164
00:05:14,881 --> 00:05:17,084
and he turned around at the door

165
00:05:17,084 --> 00:05:18,117
and saluted me.

166
00:05:18,297 --> 00:05:19,783
A real salute.

167
00:05:19,963 --> 00:05:22,408
And then the bus pulled away into

168
00:05:22,408 --> 00:05:23,074
the snow.

169
00:05:23,424 --> 00:05:25,969
I walked home from the station with

170
00:05:25,969 --> 00:05:27,060
an empty purse,

171
00:05:27,060 --> 00:05:28,951
and I cried the whole way.

172
00:05:29,131 --> 00:05:31,197
Not because I was sorry.

173
00:05:31,377 --> 00:05:33,212
Because I was scared.

174
00:05:33,392 --> 00:05:35,913
When I got home and told Harold,

175
00:05:35,913 --> 00:05:37,095
he didn't yell.

176
00:05:37,275 --> 00:05:38,401
That was worse,

177
00:05:38,401 --> 00:05:39,226
sweetheart.

178
00:05:39,406 --> 00:05:41,066
He just went quiet.

179
00:05:41,246 --> 00:05:42,293
He said, Ruth,

180
00:05:42,293 --> 00:05:44,462
what are we going to tell the

181
00:05:44,462 --> 00:05:45,135
children?

182
00:05:45,315 --> 00:05:47,532
And he went to bed without another

183
00:05:47,532 --> 00:05:47,858
word.

184
00:05:48,207 --> 00:05:51,598
Christmas morning, there was nothing under our

185
00:05:51,598 --> 00:05:53,882
tree but two oranges and a pair

186
00:05:53,882 --> 00:05:56,683
of mittens I'd knitted for each child.

187
00:05:56,863 --> 00:05:59,750
We had pancakes for Christmas dinner,

188
00:05:59,750 --> 00:06:00,218
honey.

189
00:06:00,398 --> 00:06:02,895
Pancakes and the last of the maple

190
00:06:02,895 --> 00:06:03,335
syrup.

191
00:06:03,515 --> 00:06:05,408
Mark didn't understand.

192
00:06:05,588 --> 00:06:08,270
He kept asking where Santa was.

193
00:06:08,450 --> 00:06:10,087
Susan understood.

194
00:06:10,267 --> 00:06:11,280
She was five,

195
00:06:11,280 --> 00:06:13,774
and she patted my hand and said,

196
00:06:13,774 --> 00:06:16,501
Mommy, I like pancakes best anyway.

197
00:06:16,851 --> 00:06:18,649
And then, around noon,

198
00:06:18,649 --> 00:06:21,101
there was a knock at the door.

199
00:06:21,281 --> 00:06:22,604
It was Mr.

200
00:06:22,784 --> 00:06:25,085
Kowalski from next door,

201
00:06:25,085 --> 00:06:26,139
with a ham.

202
00:06:26,319 --> 00:06:29,112
And then the Johnsons from across the

203
00:06:29,112 --> 00:06:30,546
street, with a pie.

204
00:06:30,726 --> 00:06:32,037
And Pastor Gray,

205
00:06:32,037 --> 00:06:34,250
with a bag of toys from the

206
00:06:34,250 --> 00:06:34,824
church.

207
00:06:35,004 --> 00:06:37,976
I never told anybody what happened.

208
00:06:38,156 --> 00:06:39,265
To this day,

209
00:06:39,265 --> 00:06:42,223
I think Susan went next door and

210
00:06:42,223 --> 00:06:43,055
told Mrs.

211
00:06:43,235 --> 00:06:44,547
Kowalski.

212
00:06:44,727 --> 00:06:46,109
That little girl.

213
00:06:46,289 --> 00:06:48,054
She never would admit it.

214
00:06:48,403 --> 00:06:50,231
So far, the station,

215
00:06:50,231 --> 00:06:52,516
the envelope, the salute,

216
00:06:52,516 --> 00:06:54,069
and the pancakes.

217
00:06:54,249 --> 00:06:57,140
Harold forgave me by New Year's.

218
00:06:57,320 --> 00:06:58,517
He said, Ruthie,

219
00:06:58,517 --> 00:07:00,687
I'd have done the same thing.

220
00:07:00,867 --> 00:07:03,317
I'd have just felt worse about it.

221
00:07:03,497 --> 00:07:05,052
Life went on.

222
00:07:05,232 --> 00:07:07,966
Harold got called back to the plant

223
00:07:07,966 --> 00:07:08,669
in March.

224
00:07:08,849 --> 00:07:10,556
Things got better.

225
00:07:10,736 --> 00:07:13,070
And I didn't hear one word from

226
00:07:13,070 --> 00:07:13,522
Billy.

227
00:07:13,702 --> 00:07:14,878
Not a letter,

228
00:07:14,878 --> 00:07:15,873
not a card.

229
00:07:16,053 --> 00:07:17,005
Nothing.

230
00:07:17,355 --> 00:07:19,026
Harold's brother said,

231
00:07:19,026 --> 00:07:21,608
you'll never see that money again,

232
00:07:21,608 --> 00:07:21,988
Ruth.

233
00:07:22,168 --> 00:07:25,291
Every soldier in every bus station has

234
00:07:25,291 --> 00:07:26,278
a sad story.

235
00:07:26,458 --> 00:07:28,433
I'd bet you he was drinking it

236
00:07:28,433 --> 00:07:29,948
up in a bar in Akron an

237
00:07:29,948 --> 00:07:30,672
hour later.

238
00:07:30,852 --> 00:07:31,962
And I'll admit,

239
00:07:31,962 --> 00:07:34,107
honey, as the months went by,

240
00:07:34,107 --> 00:07:36,326
a little part of me started to

241
00:07:36,326 --> 00:07:38,027
wonder if he was right.

242
00:07:38,207 --> 00:07:39,740
Not about the money.

243
00:07:39,920 --> 00:07:41,104
About Billy.

244
00:07:41,453 --> 00:07:44,227
On Christmas Eve of nineteen seventy,

245
00:07:44,227 --> 00:07:45,951
exactly one year later,

246
00:07:45,951 --> 00:07:47,900
at seven o'clock at night,

247
00:07:47,900 --> 00:07:49,924
a car pulled up in front of

248
00:07:49,924 --> 00:07:51,798
our house on Maple Drive.

249
00:07:51,978 --> 00:07:53,975
A blue station wagon.

250
00:07:54,155 --> 00:07:55,989
Susan was at the window,

251
00:07:55,989 --> 00:07:56,982
and she said,

252
00:07:56,982 --> 00:07:58,892
Mommy, there's a soldier.

253
00:07:59,072 --> 00:08:00,918
And my heart stopped.

254
00:08:01,098 --> 00:08:03,767
Because walking up our front steps,

255
00:08:03,767 --> 00:08:05,444
limping just a little,

256
00:08:05,444 --> 00:08:07,046
in his dress uniform,

257
00:08:07,046 --> 00:08:07,808
was Billy.

258
00:08:08,158 --> 00:08:09,900
And he wasn't alone.

259
00:08:10,080 --> 00:08:12,792
Behind him came a little woman in

260
00:08:12,792 --> 00:08:13,942
a church coat,

261
00:08:13,942 --> 00:08:16,901
his mother, and three teenage girls,

262
00:08:16,901 --> 00:08:19,530
his sisters, all carrying boxes.

263
00:08:19,710 --> 00:08:21,990
Billy stood at our door,

264
00:08:21,990 --> 00:08:23,605
took off his cap,

265
00:08:23,605 --> 00:08:24,935
and said, Mrs.

266
00:08:25,115 --> 00:08:27,981
Miller, I'm sorry it took me a

267
00:08:27,981 --> 00:08:28,459
year.

268
00:08:28,639 --> 00:08:30,496
I wanted to do it right.

269
00:08:30,676 --> 00:08:33,592
And then his mother pushed right past

270
00:08:33,592 --> 00:08:36,113
him and threw her arms around my

271
00:08:36,113 --> 00:08:38,083
neck and wouldn't let go.

272
00:08:38,433 --> 00:08:41,091
She said, you brought my boy home

273
00:08:41,091 --> 00:08:42,218
for Christmas.

274
00:08:42,398 --> 00:08:44,372
Do you know what that meant?

275
00:08:44,552 --> 00:08:46,783
His father died the year before he

276
00:08:46,783 --> 00:08:47,571
shipped out.

277
00:08:47,751 --> 00:08:49,885
I'd prayed every night for two years

278
00:08:49,885 --> 00:08:52,197
that he'd come through that door again.

279
00:08:52,377 --> 00:08:54,281
And on Christmas morning,

280
00:08:54,281 --> 00:08:55,271
there he was,

281
00:08:55,271 --> 00:08:57,555
with a box of chocolates and a

282
00:08:57,555 --> 00:08:58,926
bottle of perfume,

283
00:08:58,926 --> 00:09:01,135
and a little doll for each of

284
00:09:01,135 --> 00:09:02,048
his sisters.

285
00:09:02,228 --> 00:09:03,944
He told me a lady at the

286
00:09:03,944 --> 00:09:05,874
bus station gave it to him.

287
00:09:06,223 --> 00:09:08,209
The boxes, sweetheart.

288
00:09:08,389 --> 00:09:11,150
A ham, the biggest one I've ever

289
00:09:11,150 --> 00:09:11,582
seen.

290
00:09:11,762 --> 00:09:13,817
Two pies his mother had baked.

291
00:09:13,997 --> 00:09:16,205
A doll for Susan with a blue

292
00:09:16,205 --> 00:09:16,678
dress.

293
00:09:16,858 --> 00:09:18,159
And for Mark,

294
00:09:18,159 --> 00:09:20,260
a red toy fire truck.

295
00:09:20,440 --> 00:09:23,157
Not the little one from the five-and-dime.

296
00:09:23,337 --> 00:09:24,605
A big metal one,

297
00:09:24,605 --> 00:09:26,982
with a ladder that went up and

298
00:09:26,982 --> 00:09:28,329
a bell that rang.

299
00:09:28,509 --> 00:09:31,454
Mark rang that bell until Harold hid

300
00:09:31,454 --> 00:09:33,827
it in the closet in February.

301
00:09:34,177 --> 00:09:37,021
And then Billy handed me an envelope.

302
00:09:37,201 --> 00:09:39,485
A plain white envelope,

303
00:09:39,485 --> 00:09:40,974
just like mine.

304
00:09:41,154 --> 00:09:43,705
Inside was a twenty-dollar bill,

305
00:09:43,705 --> 00:09:44,742
and a letter.

306
00:09:44,922 --> 00:09:46,991
He'd gotten a job at the tire

307
00:09:46,991 --> 00:09:49,345
factory in Akron when he got out.

308
00:09:49,525 --> 00:09:52,014
He said he'd put aside one dollar

309
00:09:52,014 --> 00:09:53,221
every two weeks,

310
00:09:53,221 --> 00:09:55,182
all year, in a coffee can,

311
00:09:55,182 --> 00:09:57,293
so it would be his own money

312
00:09:57,293 --> 00:09:58,651
that paid me back,

313
00:09:58,651 --> 00:09:59,556
not a check.

314
00:09:59,736 --> 00:10:01,385
He wanted it to be real.

315
00:10:01,735 --> 00:10:05,183
Then he stood up straight and said,

316
00:10:05,183 --> 00:10:05,577
Mrs.

317
00:10:05,757 --> 00:10:08,037
Miller, I was sitting in that station

318
00:10:08,037 --> 00:10:10,502
thinking about whether it was even worth

319
00:10:10,502 --> 00:10:11,179
going home.

320
00:10:11,359 --> 00:10:13,797
I'd seen some things over there.

321
00:10:13,977 --> 00:10:15,852
I didn't think I could be a

322
00:10:15,852 --> 00:10:17,449
regular person anymore.

323
00:10:17,629 --> 00:10:20,564
And then a stranger gave me everything

324
00:10:20,564 --> 00:10:21,181
she had.

325
00:10:21,361 --> 00:10:23,585
I figured if people like you are

326
00:10:23,585 --> 00:10:26,156
out here, then it's worth being home.

327
00:10:26,336 --> 00:10:29,070
I've never forgotten those words,

328
00:10:29,070 --> 00:10:29,982
sweetheart.

329
00:10:30,162 --> 00:10:31,160
Not one.

330
00:10:31,511 --> 00:10:34,657
Billy and his family came every Christmas

331
00:10:34,657 --> 00:10:36,576
Eve for twenty-two years,

332
00:10:36,576 --> 00:10:37,037
honey.

333
00:10:37,217 --> 00:10:39,479
He married a girl named Carol,

334
00:10:39,479 --> 00:10:42,269
became a foreman at the tire factory,

335
00:10:42,269 --> 00:10:43,927
and had four children.

336
00:10:44,107 --> 00:10:45,741
When Harold passed,

337
00:10:45,741 --> 00:10:47,718
Billy was a pallbearer.

338
00:10:47,898 --> 00:10:49,837
And when Billy passed,

339
00:10:49,837 --> 00:10:51,952
in two thousand fifteen,

340
00:10:51,952 --> 00:10:54,156
his oldest son called me.

341
00:10:54,336 --> 00:10:56,194
He said, Mrs.

342
00:10:56,374 --> 00:10:59,311
Miller, Dad asked that you have this.

343
00:10:59,491 --> 00:11:01,352
It was the bus ticket,

344
00:11:01,352 --> 00:11:01,859
honey.

345
00:11:02,039 --> 00:11:04,083
My address still on the back.

346
00:11:04,433 --> 00:11:07,189
I still have that twenty-dollar bill,

347
00:11:07,189 --> 00:11:08,008
sweetheart.

348
00:11:08,188 --> 00:11:09,802
I never spent it.

349
00:11:09,982 --> 00:11:12,048
It's in a frame on my kitchen

350
00:11:12,048 --> 00:11:14,115
wall, next to the bus ticket,

351
00:11:14,115 --> 00:11:15,183
and the letter.

352
00:11:15,363 --> 00:11:16,768
Every Christmas Eve,

353
00:11:16,768 --> 00:11:18,664
I take it down and read the

354
00:11:18,664 --> 00:11:21,192
letter out loud to my grandchildren.

355
00:11:21,372 --> 00:11:23,890
And I tell them what I'm telling

356
00:11:23,890 --> 00:11:24,204
you.

357
00:11:24,384 --> 00:11:27,379
The best twenty dollars you'll ever spend

358
00:11:27,379 --> 00:11:29,789
is the twenty you can't afford to

359
00:11:29,789 --> 00:11:30,155
give.

360
00:11:30,505 --> 00:11:32,529
Tell me in the comments,

361
00:11:32,529 --> 00:11:33,036
honey.

362
00:11:33,216 --> 00:11:35,356
Would you have given away your last

363
00:11:35,356 --> 00:11:37,314
twenty dollars on Christmas Eve?

364
00:11:37,494 --> 00:11:39,433
Be honest with Grandma.

365
00:11:39,613 --> 00:11:41,708
And if you or someone you love

366
00:11:41,708 --> 00:11:43,734
served, I want to thank them.

367
00:11:43,914 --> 00:11:46,219
There are more of my family's stories

368
00:11:46,219 --> 00:11:48,400
in the playlist right there on your

369
00:11:48,400 --> 00:11:50,331
screen, and I'll see you in the

370
00:11:50,331 --> 00:11:50,892
next one.

371
00:11:51,072 --> 00:11:52,871
Good night, sweetheart.

372
00:11:53,051 --> 00:11:54,491
Merry Christmas.
